  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Def6 replaces set equality with subset inclusion, asserting `∃ F, F.RE ∧ K ⊆ F.theory` instead of `∃ F, F.RE ∧ K = F.theory` (i.e., `Def5 K F`). This fails to capture Koellner's WMT (`∃e (K = F_e)`, that a machine and the idealized mind have the same outputs) and the author's claim ('K is the theorem set of some machine'). Moreover, because no consistency or soundness condition is placed on `F`, any inconsistent recursively enumerable axiom set (such as `{⊥}`) proves every sentence, making `Def6 K` trivially true for every `K`."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def1 (and hence Def5 and Def6) fixes the domain to sentences of first-order arithmetic (`ℒₒᵣ`) under classical first-order derivability, whereas the quoted passages speak more generally of 'sentences' without explicitly restricting the language."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "While Def1 and Def5 reasonably represent K as a set of arithmetic sentences and the relation `K = F.theory`, Def6 contains a major divergence: it weakens `K = F.theory` to `K ⊆ F.theory`, which fails to express Koellner's WMT and becomes trivially true for every K via any inconsistent recursively enumerable theory."
}
